# an alternative way to amplify is through distortion. eg.,
.distort("3:.5")

# infinite riser. room and gain are extras, very flexible. don't be afraid to quickly start and stop to get the effect again.
:$ s("pulse!8").fm(time).fmh(time).room(1).gain(.6)

# use .clip() with longer samples like "pad" to prevent overstacking.
# .clip(1) is one cycle long.

# .postgain() is nice because it's last in the stack. changes volume after everything else, so easier to control than .gain()

# S$: is like _$: but instead of mute, it solos the track. It's a little weird when you realize this is just function naming. You can combine S and _ with any track name, and might be surprised that a track starting with a capital S gets soloed (e.g., Silence:).

# .transpose() lets you shift by semitones instead of by scale notes. trans() is alias

# you don't have to use .duckdepth(). .duckattack(), etc. are also available and attack changes how quickly the sound comes back in.

# bear in mind, though we don't have to get too creative just yet, there's not limit to how sidechaining (orbit) can be used.

# room and delay tend to work well together. usually, you don't want to go above room(1).

# sbd is NOT a sample. it's a synthesized base drum (sound font / stynth).

# An important concept that can explain some problems I face: https://youtu.be/m7Rp46AU8dw?si=lhYpKVn4SYyyc0mD&t=1242
# .set.mix(stuff here)
# setDefaultJoin('mix') is a thing, but stay away from this for now. It affects interpretation of more than just set(), but it can certainly be useful once I have the core of strudel down.

# you can log to console with .log() (this makes it more liks sonic pi)

# lpe is short for lp envelope
# lpe(n) uses 2^n (maybe not super important)

# resonance lpq(8) is a fine place to start

# sometimes, rarely, almostNever, never (never might actually be useful for live coding)
# `sometimesBy(n, x => x.stuff)`, n is a prob [0,1].

# You can basically just replace n() with i() for .xen and it will work. The important thing to understand is that xen uses frequency space, not log (note value) space and i() makes that conversion.

# In the future, predefine my own xenscales and also learn to scale perlin so that it jumps the right amount.

